<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="53fb4810" lines="105-119">
<description>
Extend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.
- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.

Rule:
- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.
- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.
- '+' pixels are never overwritten.

Key insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="89565ca0" lines="120-136">
<description>
Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.

Input structure:
- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.
- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.
- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.

Rule:
- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.
- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.
- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.
- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.

Key insight:
- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="652646ff" lines="84-105">
<description>
Recover each partially hidden 6x6 ring sprite and stack complete copies from top layer to bottom layer.

Input structure:
- The dominant color is the background.
- Several non-background colors are visible fragments of the same 12-pixel
  outline ring in a 6x6 box; small off-template marker pixels are noise.
- Ring fragments overlap, so upper rings replace some expected pixels of
  lower rings, and a ring may also be clipped by the input boundary.

Rule:
- Identify every substantial color whose pixels all lie on one translated
  copy of the ring template.
- For each missing in-bounds template pixel, the color currently occupying
  that pixel is above the hidden ring.
- Topologically sort these occlusion relations from top to bottom, then draw
  one complete 6x6 ring for each color in that order on a background canvas.

Key insight:
- The output is not a crop of the scene; it is the layer stack of complete
  ring sprites reconstructed from their visible fragments.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="79cce52d" lines="29-44">
<description>
Crop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.

Input structure
- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.
- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.
- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.

Rule
- Remove the top control row and left control column.
- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.
- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.

Key insight
- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="3d6c6e23" lines="10-27">
<description>
Move each vertical stream of pixels into a bottom-aligned centered pile.

Input structure
- A mostly background grid contains one or more vertical source columns of
  foreground pixels.
- Each source column has n^2 foreground pixels, and the top-to-bottom color
  sequence splits into monochrome groups of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...

Rule
- Clear the canvas to background.
- For each source column, read the foreground colors from top to bottom and
  rebuild them at the bottom below the same column as odd-width centered
  rows of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...

Key insight
- The scattered vertical pixels are falling grains; a square number of
  grains decomposes into the odd row widths of a centered pyramid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="bd283c4a" lines="7-23">
<description>
Pack all pixels into color-frequency runs along bottom-up columns.

Input structure
The input is a rectangular grid whose colors are all foreground material;
only the number of pixels of each color matters. In the observed task
instances, every present color has a distinct pixel count.

Rule
Count the pixels of each color and order the colors from most frequent to
least frequent. Rewrite a same-size grid by placing each color's full count
as one contiguous run while scanning columns from left to right and, within
each column, from bottom to top.

Key insight
The output preserves the multiset of colors but discards all original
positions, turning the grid into a frequency-sorted column packing.

